{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Perfect Square Trinomial.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Perfect Square Trinomial.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"perfect square trinomial perfect square trinomial","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Perfect Square Trinomial.md","fileName":"Perfect Square Trinomial.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Perfect Square Trinomial","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 23:41\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Quadratic Equations]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Quadratic Equations","Completing the Square","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Perfect Square Trinomial.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Perfect Square Trinomial.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"perfect square trinomial perfect square trinomial","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Perfect Square Trinomial.md","fileName":"Perfect Square Trinomial.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Perfect Square Trinomial","heading":"Perfect Square Trinomial","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A perfect square trinomial is a three-term polynomial that is the square of a binomial:\n\n$$a^2+2ab+b^2=(a+b)^2,$$\n\nor\n\n$$a^2-2ab+b^2=(a-b)^2.$$\n\nIts first and last terms are squares, and its middle term is twice the product of their square roots with the appropriate sign. Recognizing this structure permits immediate factorization. [[Completing the Square]] creates the structure when the original quadratic lacks the needed constant term.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Quadratic Equations","Completing the Square","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Perfect Square Trinomial.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Perfect Square Trinomial.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSear